[1mStack[0m
    dup          x -- x x                           duplicate top of stack
    drop         x --                               drop the top stack element
    swap         a b -- b a                         swap top two elements
    over         a b -- a b a                       copy second item to top
    nip          a b -- b                           drop second-from-top element (keep top)
    tuck         a b -- b a b                       duplicate second item and tuck below top
    roll         ... n -- ...                       move the item at depth n to the top (1 roll = swap, 2 roll = rot)
    pick         ... n -- ... v                     copy the item at depth n to the top (0 pick = dup)
    depth        -- n                               number of items on the stack
    clst         ... --                             clear the stack
    savestack    --                                 save the whole stack to stack_backup.dat in the config directory
    loadstack    -- ...                             push the stack saved by savestack


[1mwords[0m
A word is a command you define: : name body ;  e.g.  : sq dup * ;  then  5 sq  gives 25.
Words run like built-in commands, can call other words and themselves, and are what the
numerical methods take as functions (@sq pushes a token for it). Inside a word:
  flag if ... else ... then      begin ... flag until      begin ... flag while ... repeat
  limit start do ... loop        i (loop index)  j        n +loop
listwords shows yours, "name" delword deletes one, clrwords all; savewords writes them
to ~/.config/mm41/user_words.txt, which loads at startup. A word is one line; put a
family of them in a word module instead (help modules). Manual: chapter Words.


[1msyntax[0m
Tokens are separated by spaces; numbers go on the stack, commands take their arguments
from it (3 4 + is 7). Numbers 3 -1.2e-4 .5, decimals 0.1d, complex (1,2), strings "text",
matrices [rows cols $ a11 a12 ...] or [rows, cols, "file"]. Comments start with \ or #.
The stack prints after every line, [0] at the bottom (or as an HP-41, HP-12C or HP-48 display
would show it: skin hp41, skin hp12c, skin hp48, skin hp48px; skin plain); undo restores the previous
stack, q quits. help <name> describes any command; manual <chapter or word> opens the manual there;
tutor walks you through the calculator a step at a time, examples <chapter> prints the same as a cookbook.
Matrices: [1 2 3] is a row vector, [1 2; 3 4] a 2 x 2 matrix (a semicolon ends a row), and the
older [rows cols $ elements] form is what the calculator writes back.
Commands that take a name take a string: "const" use, "us" usehol, "f.so" load. A line
that is only such a command (use unuse load run batch usehol) and one bare name means
the same: use const, usehol us. Anything longer is evaluated as written.
Manual: chapter Entering data.


[1mnumerics[0m
The numerical methods take a function as an execution token: @name, for a word or a command.
  a b @f integrate     a b @f romberg      a b @f fzero       x @f solve
  a b @f minimize      x @f deriv          lo hi @f fsum      lo hi @f fprod     @f exec
The function is usually a word of your own, defined on its own line; it takes x from the
stack and leaves f(x):   : f dup * 2 - ;   0 3 @f fzero  -> 1.414214     0 3 @f integrate  -> 3
A function with a parameter: fix the parameter inside the word, or keep it in a register.
  : j2 2 besselj ;   0 10 @j2 integrate  -> 0.980066      (besselj is x n -- Jn(x); n fixed at 2)
  2 0 sto   : jn 0 rcl besselj ;   0 10 @jn integrate       (n in register 0; 3 0 sto changes it)
  : k 0 rcl * sin ;   0 pi @k integrate                   (sin kx with k in register 0)
Built-ins and words from word modules work the same way: @sin, @exp, @npdf. fsum and fprod
call the word with the integer i; odeint and odesol with t y (help odeint); sims with nothing.
help <command> shows an example with a word of your own for every command that takes @f.
x tol sets the tolerance (kept in config.txt); nevals and interr report the last call.
Manual: chapter Numerical methods.


[1mmodule numerics 1.0[0m   integration, roots, minima, derivatives and sums over any word
    exec         xt --                              run an execution token (@name pushes one)
    integrate    a b xt -- I                        integral of f over [a,b] (tanh-sinh with adaptive bisection; handles endpoint singularities)
    romberg      a b xt -- I                        integral of f over [a,b] by Romberg extrapolation
    fzero        a b xt -- x                        root of f in the bracket [a,b] where f changes sign (Brent)
    solve        guess xt -- x                      root of f near one guess (bracket search, then Brent)
    minimize     a b xt -- x                        argument of the minimum of f in [a,b] (Brent)
    deriv        x xt -- f'(x)                      derivative of f at x (Ridders extrapolation)
    fsum         lo hi xt -- S                      sum of f(i) for integer i from lo to hi
    fprod        lo hi xt -- P                      product of f(i) for integer i from lo to hi
    tol          x --                               set the convergence tolerance (default 1e-10)
    nevals       -- n                               function evaluations used by the last numerical call
    interr       -- e                               error estimate of the last integrate
    'help <name>' for any of them


[1mintegrate[0m   a b xt -- I   [module numerics, Numerics]
    integral of f over [a,b] (tanh-sinh with adaptive bisection; handles endpoint singularities)
    takes 3 items (the last one named is on top):
      a          a real number
      b          a real number
      xt         an execution token: @word or @command, for a word of your own as much as a built-in (help numerics)
    leaves 1 item:
      I          a real number, the integral
    example:  0 pi @sin integrate
    with a word of your own (define it on its own line, then use @name):
      the word is  x -- f(x)
      : f dup * 2 - ;   0 3 @f integrate
      -> 3   (x^2 - 2 over [0, 3])
    a function with a parameter: fix the parameter inside the word (besselj is x n -- Jn(x); here n = 2)
      : j2 2 besselj ;   0 10 @j2 integrate     -> 0.980066
      2 0 sto   : jn 0 rcl besselj ;             the same with n in register 0; 3 0 sto changes it
    a word from a word module or a built-in command works the same way: @sin, @npdf
    see also: exec romberg fzero solve minimize deriv fsum fprod tol nevals interr 


[1mfzero[0m   a b xt -- x   [module numerics, Numerics]
    root of f in the bracket [a,b] where f changes sign (Brent)
    takes 3 items (the last one named is on top):
      a          a real number
      b          a real number
      xt         an execution token: @word or @command, for a word of your own as much as a built-in (help numerics)
    leaves 1 item:
      x          a number: real, or complex, decimal or matrix where allowed
    example:  2 4 @sin fzero
    with a word of your own (define it on its own line, then use @name):
      the word is  x -- f(x)
      : f dup * 2 - ;   0 3 @f fzero
      -> 1.414214
    a function with a parameter: fix the parameter inside the word (besselj is x n -- Jn(x); here n = 2)
      : j2 2 besselj ;   4 7 @j2 fzero          -> 5.135622   (the second zero of J2)
      2 0 sto   : jn 0 rcl besselj ;             the same with n in register 0; 3 0 sto changes it
    a word from a word module or a built-in command works the same way: @sin, @npdf
    see also: exec integrate romberg solve minimize deriv fsum fprod tol nevals interr 


[1msims[0m   n xt -- v   [module random, Random]
    run a word n times, collecting the one real it leaves each time ( -- x ) into a column
    takes 2 items (the last one named is on top):
      n          a whole number (real or decimal)
      xt         an execution token: @word or @command, for a word of your own as much as a built-in (help numerics)
    leaves 1 item:
      v          a vector: an N x 1 or 1 x N real matrix
    example:  : pnl 0 1 rnorm 100 * ; 10000 @pnl sims mcstats
    with a word of your own (define it on its own line, then use @name):
      the word is  -- x
      : coin 0 1 runif 0 round ;   1000 @coin sims mean
      -> about 0.5   (the share of heads)
    a parameter the word needs: fix it inside the word or keep it in a register (help numerics)
    a word from a word module or a built-in command works the same way: @sin, @npdf
    see also: seed rnorm runif rpois rexp normrnd unifrnd poisrnd binomrnd exprnd trnd chi2rnd 
              resample shuffle mcstats 


[1modesol[0m   y0 t0 t1 xt -- y   [module ode, Differential equations]
    the state at t1 only
    takes 4 items (the last one named is on top):
      y0         the initial state: a real, or a row vector for a system
      t0         a real number, the start
      t1         a real number, the end
      xt         an execution token: @word or @command, for a word of your own as much as a built-in (help numerics)
    leaves 1 item:
      y          a number: real, or complex, decimal or matrix where allowed
    example:  : f swap drop chs ; 1 0 1 @f odesol
    with a word of your own (define it on its own line, then use @name):
      the word is  t y -- y'
      : lg swap drop dup 1 swap - * ;   0.1 0 5 @lg odesol
      -> 0.942826   (the logistic curve at t = 5)
    a parameter the word needs: fix it inside the word or keep it in a register (help numerics)
    a word from a word module or a built-in command works the same way: @sin, @npdf
    see also: odeint 


[1mfplot[0m   a b xt --   [module plot, Plot]
    draw the word (x -- y) over [a, b], one point per pixel column; points where it fails are left out
    takes 3 items (the last one named is on top):
      a          a real number
      b          a real number
      xt         an execution token: @word or @command, for a word of your own as much as a built-in (help numerics)
    leaves nothing
    example:  0 10 @sin fplot
    with a word of your own (define it on its own line, then use @name):
      the word is  x -- y
      : f dup * 2 - ;   -2 2 @f fplot
      -> the parabola, drawn
    a function with a parameter: fix the parameter inside the word (besselj is x n -- Jn(x); here n = 2)
      : j2 2 besselj ;   0 20 @j2 fplot         -> J2 over [0, 20], drawn
      2 0 sto   : jn 0 rcl besselj ;             the same with n in register 0; 3 0 sto changes it
    a word from a word module or a built-in command works the same way: @sin, @npdf
    see also: plot scatter steps bars hplot mplot hold nohold show pclear ptitle pxlabel 
              pylabel plegend pxlog pylog plin pgrid pxrange pyrange pauto psize psvg ptxt 


commands mentioning "holiday":
    workdays     d1 d2 -- n                         workdays from d1 to d2, both inclusive (Excel NETWORKDAYS): Mondays to Fridays not in the holiday calendar; negative if d2 < d1
    isworkday    date -- flag                       1 if the date is a Monday to Friday and not a holiday
    holidays     name -- H                          the holiday calendar as a vector of YYYYMMDD: the file holidays/name.txt in the config directory, or the built-in rules "us" (federal, observed) and "si" (Slovenia) for the years around today
    yholidays    year name -- H                     the built-in rules for one year
    sethol       H --                               install a vector of dates YYYYMMDD as the holiday calendar for this session
    clrhol       --                                 remove the holiday calendar (weekends only) and forget it in config.txt
    hol          -- H                               the installed holiday calendar as a vector

